import{_ as t,o as s,c as i,a2 as a}from"./chunks/framework.BM5YPQmL.js";const k=JSON.parse('{"title":"AI integration","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"guide/ai.md","filePath":"guide/ai.md"}'),o={name:"guide/ai.md"};function d(n,e,r,l,h,c){return s(),i("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[a(`<h1 id="ai-integration" tabindex="-1">AI integration <a class="header-anchor" href="#ai-integration" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;AI integration&quot;">​</a></h1><p>ReelForge works on its own: you write the script, design scenes in the editor and render. If you use an AI assistant, it can do the heavy lifting: write the script, build every scene, look at rendered frames and fix problems. You stay in control. You record your own voice, check the cues by ear and approve the result in the editor.</p><p>The render engine contains no AI: it draws every frame deterministically from <code>episode.yaml</code>. Two optional helpers run on your computer:</p><ul><li><strong>Speech alignment</strong> (<code>align_cues</code>, <code>reelforge align</code>) marks every line and word from <code>voice.wav</code>. It works from pause detection alone; <code>reelforge align --setup</code> downloads the optional speech models (Whisper and Silero VAD) once, and after that alignment runs offline.</li><li><strong>Scene suggestions</strong> (<code>suggest_scene</code>, <code>suggest_scenes</code>) use a built-in keyword dictionary, or a language model you configure (see <a href="#models">Local and cloud models</a>).</li></ul><h2 id="three-ways-in" tabindex="-1">Three ways in <a class="header-anchor" href="#three-ways-in" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Three ways in&quot;">​</a></h2><p>All three serve the same tools from one registry, so names, arguments and results are identical (ADR-014). The <a href="./../reference/mcp-tools.html">MCP tools reference</a> lists every tool.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Transport</th><th>Start it</th><th>Endpoint</th><th>Use it for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>MCP over stdio</td><td>your client runs <code>reelforge mcp</code></td><td>standard input/output</td><td>Claude Code, Cursor, VS Code, Codex, Gemini CLI</td></tr><tr><td>MCP Streamable HTTP</td><td><code>reelforge studio</code> (keep it running)</td><td><code>http://127.0.0.1:4310/mcp</code></td><td>clients that prefer a URL, several clients</td></tr><tr><td>REST and OpenAPI</td><td><code>reelforge studio</code></td><td><code>http://127.0.0.1:4310/api/v1</code></td><td>agent frameworks with function calling</td></tr></tbody></table><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">./reelforge</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> mcp</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">          # macOS / Linux</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">reelforge.cmd</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> mcp</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">        # Windows</span></span></code></pre></div><p>You don&#39;t run the stdio server yourself: your client starts it when needed. It runs on your computer and never sends your files anywhere; the AI service only sees what your client sends it.</p><p>The REST API answers <code>GET /api/v1/tools</code> (add <code>?format=openai</code> for OpenAI function definitions), <code>POST /api/v1/tools/&lt;name&gt;</code> with the arguments as JSON, and <code>GET /api/v1/openapi.json</code> for GPT Actions and other OpenAPI importers. The dashboard also serves <code>/.well-known/mcp.json</code> (a server card) and <code>/llms.txt</code> / <code>/llms-full.txt</code> (the documentation in one file).</p><h2 id="client-setup" tabindex="-1">Client setup <a class="header-anchor" href="#client-setup" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Client setup&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The project folder already contains configuration for Claude Code (<code>.mcp.json</code>), Cursor (<code>.cursor/mcp.json</code>), VS Code (<code>.vscode/mcp.json</code>) and Gemini CLI (<code>.gemini/settings.json</code>). Open the folder in the client and approve the <code>reelforge</code> server. For example, with Claude Code:</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">claude</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> mcp</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> add</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> reelforge</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> node</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> packages/cli/bin/reelforge.js</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> mcp</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Try: <em>&quot;Make a 40-second English episode about database indexes, preview it and fix any issues.&quot;</em></p><p>Every other client (Claude Desktop, Windsurf, Codex CLI, Zed, Continue, LM Studio and any OpenAI-compatible framework), in stdio and HTTP variants, is in <a href="./../reference/ai-clients.html">AI client setup</a>. The dashboard&#39;s <strong>Connect AI</strong> page (<code>/connect</code>) shows the same snippets with your real paths and a connection test.</p><h2 id="toolsets" tabindex="-1">Toolsets <a class="header-anchor" href="#toolsets" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Toolsets&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Small context windows work better with fewer tools. Ask for one or more toolsets:</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">reelforge</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> mcp</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --toolsets</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> basic,design</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Over HTTP use <code>/mcp?toolsets=basic</code> (also on <code>/api/v1/tools</code> and <code>/api/v1/openapi.json</code>).</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Toolset</th><th>Contains</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>basic</code></td><td>read, create (from a script or a template), add scenes, validate, preview, cues</td></tr><tr><td><code>design</code></td><td>structured edits, full YAML, component schemas, blueprints and scene suggestions</td></tr><tr><td><code>render</code></td><td>render, status, cancel, open the editor, environment check</td></tr><tr><td><code>all</code></td><td>every tool (the default)</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="tool-tiers" tabindex="-1">Tool tiers <a class="header-anchor" href="#tool-tiers" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Tool tiers&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Pick the tools that match the model&#39;s strength. Every write tool returns the validation summary, one fix hint per problem (<code>fixes</code>) and the recommended next step (<code>next</code>), so any model can correct itself in a loop.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Tier</th><th>For</th><th>Tools</th><th>The model writes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>small models</td><td><code>list_templates</code> → <code>create_from_template</code>, <code>suggest_scenes</code></td><td>a flat object of slot values; no YAML</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>medium models</td><td><code>create_episode</code>, <code>list_blueprints</code> → <code>add_scene</code></td><td>the script and a few fields per scene</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>strong models</td><td><code>edit_episode</code>, <code>write_episode_yaml</code>, <code>get_component_schema</code></td><td>any element, prop, time and animation</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Shared tools for every tier: <code>get_episode</code>, <code>validate_episode</code>, <code>preview_frames</code> (images for vision models plus a text layout description for text-only models), <code>draft_cues</code>, <code>align_cues</code>, <code>set_cues</code>, <code>render_episode</code>, <code>get_render_status</code>, <code>cancel_render</code>, <code>open_editor</code>, <code>doctor</code> and <code>get_guide</code>.</p><h2 id="skills-and-instruction-files" tabindex="-1">Skills and instruction files <a class="header-anchor" href="#skills-and-instruction-files" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Skills and instruction files&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Task guides live in <code>skills/</code> in the Agent Skills format (one folder per skill with <code>SKILL.md</code> and <code>references/</code>). Clients that support skills load them directly; others call <code>get_guide</code> (<code>topic</code>, and <code>detail: true</code> for the full guide). <code>.claude-plugin/</code> packages the skills with the MCP server as a Claude Code plugin.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Skill</th><th>Covers</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>reelforge-make-video</code></td><td>the end-to-end workflow and which tool tier to use</td></tr><tr><td><code>reelforge-script-writing</code></td><td>hooks, one idea per line, words per second, Arabic terms</td></tr><tr><td><code>reelforge-scene-design</code></td><td>layout, safe zones, component choice, motion rules</td></tr><tr><td><code>reelforge-timing</code></td><td>cues, word anchors, automatic alignment</td></tr><tr><td><code>reelforge-industry-playbooks</code></td><td>audience, tone, templates and compliance per industry</td></tr><tr><td><code>reelforge-brand-and-themes</code></td><td>themes from brand colors and fonts, contrast</td></tr><tr><td><code>reelforge-review-and-render</code></td><td>the validation loop, visual QA, render options, publishing</td></tr><tr><td><code>reelforge-contribute-component</code></td><td>adding a component correctly</td></tr></tbody></table><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Instruction file</th><th>Read by</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>AGENTS.md</code></td><td>Codex, Cursor, Copilot, Windsurf, Zed, Aider, Jules and most agents</td></tr><tr><td><code>CLAUDE.md</code></td><td>Claude Code (imports AGENTS.md)</td></tr><tr><td><code>GEMINI.md</code></td><td>Gemini CLI (imports AGENTS.md)</td></tr><tr><td><code>.github/copilot-instructions.md</code></td><td>GitHub Copilot</td></tr><tr><td><code>.cursor/rules/reelforge.mdc</code></td><td>Cursor</td></tr><tr><td><code>.windsurf/rules/reelforge.md</code></td><td>Windsurf</td></tr><tr><td><code>llms.txt</code></td><td>Assistants that look for an llms.txt index</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Edit them to add your house style, for example your series&#39; colors, tone of voice or standard ending.</p><h2 id="models" tabindex="-1">Local and cloud models <a class="header-anchor" href="#models" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Local and cloud models {#models}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>ReelForge&#39;s own scene suggestions can use a language model. Nothing is configured by default; without a model, suggestions come from the keyword dictionary. Set environment variables, or write <code>llm.json</code> in the ReelForge config folder (<code>%APPDATA%\\reelforge</code>, <code>~/Library/Application Support/reelforge</code> or <code>~/.config/reelforge</code>) with the same keys (<code>provider</code>, <code>baseUrl</code>, <code>model</code>, <code>apiKey</code>).</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Variable</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>REELFORGE_LLM_PROVIDER</code></td><td><code>openai</code> (any OpenAI-compatible server), <code>anthropic</code> or <code>ollama</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>REELFORGE_LLM_BASE_URL</code></td><td>Server URL; the provider is guessed from it when not set</td></tr><tr><td><code>REELFORGE_LLM_MODEL</code></td><td>Model name</td></tr><tr><td><code>REELFORGE_LLM_API_KEY</code></td><td>API key for cloud services (stored only in your environment or file)</td></tr></tbody></table><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># Local model through Ollama (default address 127.0.0.1:11434)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">REELFORGE_LLM_PROVIDER</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">ollama</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> REELFORGE_LLM_MODEL</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">qwen2.5:1.5b</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;"> reelforge</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> studio</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># LM Studio, llama.cpp server, vLLM or any OpenAI-compatible endpoint</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">REELFORGE_LLM_BASE_URL</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">http://127.0.0.1:1234/v1</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> REELFORGE_LLM_MODEL</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">my-model</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;"> reelforge</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> studio</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># A cloud API</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">REELFORGE_LLM_PROVIDER</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">anthropic</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> REELFORGE_LLM_API_KEY</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">...</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;"> reelforge</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> studio</span></span></code></pre></div><p>On Windows PowerShell set variables with <code>$env:REELFORGE_LLM_MODEL = &quot;qwen2.5:1.5b&quot;</code> before the command. Answers are constrained to the component schemas (JSON Schema output where the server supports it, a grammar on Ollama, otherwise a validate-and-repair loop). The dashboard shows which model is in use.</p><h2 id="evaluating-models" tabindex="-1">Evaluating models <a class="header-anchor" href="#evaluating-models" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Evaluating models&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>scripts/eval-agents.mjs</code> runs one authoring task per industry against a model through the real tool registry and scores each produced episode: created, valid, no overlaps, inside the safe zones, right language, every line covered (and, with <code>--render</code>, renders).</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">pnpm</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> build</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">node</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> scripts/eval-agents.mjs</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --mock</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">                                   # scripted model, offline (CI)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">node</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> scripts/eval-agents.mjs</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --base-url</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> http://127.0.0.1:11434/v1</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --model</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> qwen2.5:7b</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">node</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> scripts/eval-agents.mjs</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --base-url</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> https://api.example.com/v1</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --model</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> my-model</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --api-key</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">$KEY</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> \\</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">  --toolsets</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> basic</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --tasks</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> healthcare,finance</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --out</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> results.md</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The endpoint must speak OpenAI-compatible Chat Completions with tool calling. Publish results with this table (the script writes it with <code>--out</code>):</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Toolsets</th><th>Industry</th><th>Lang</th><th>Score</th><th>Calls</th><th>Tool errors</th><th>Time (s)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td></td><td>basic</td><td>…</td><td>en</td><td>%</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td></td><td>basic</td><td><strong>all</strong></td><td></td><td><strong>%</strong></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="security" tabindex="-1">Security <a class="header-anchor" href="#security" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Security {#security}&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>The dashboard and editor bind to <code>127.0.0.1</code>. Every request must carry a loopback <code>Host</code> (or a name in <code>REELFORGE_ALLOWED_HOSTS</code>), which blocks DNS rebinding; no CORS headers are sent and writes are JSON-only.</li><li><code>reelforge studio --host &lt;address&gt;</code> (or <code>REELFORGE_HOST</code>) binds elsewhere. Then <code>/mcp</code> and <code>/api/v1</code> require <code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;</code>, and other computers can reach only the AI endpoints, never the dashboard UI. <code>REELFORGE_REQUIRE_TOKEN=1</code> requires the token on loopback too.</li><li>The token is created on first use and stored in the config folder (<code>token</code>, readable only by you where the system supports it). The Connect AI page shows it only to a browser on the same computer.</li><li>Plain HTTP carries the token in clear text: on an untrusted network, put the dashboard behind a TLS proxy.</li><li>Tools only read and write inside the project folder; renders are served only from <code>out/</code>.</li></ul><p>ADR-015 records the full design.</p><h2 id="tips" tabindex="-1">Tips <a class="header-anchor" href="#tips" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Tips&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Ask the assistant to look.</strong> Say <em>&quot;preview the frames and fix anything that looks off&quot;</em>. The assistant receives the frames as images.</li><li><strong>Keep the voice human.</strong> ReelForge never generates voices. Record yourself, let <code>align_cues</code> mark the lines and words, then listen through once in the editor&#39;s <strong>Cues</strong> mode and fix any line by ear.</li><li><strong>Review in the editor.</strong> Ask for <code>open_editor</code> to get a link. Everything the assistant wrote is editable visually, and every change can be undone.</li><li><strong>Large changes:</strong> the assistant can rewrite the whole <code>episode.yaml</code>. The file is checked before it is saved, and invalid YAML is refused.</li></ul>`,44)])])}const g=t(o,[["render",d]]);export{k as __pageData,g as default};
